## Context

`tickets/AGENTS.md` is resident in every session of every project. It states:

> ID allocation is optimistic (git-erg#282, wontfix): `erg new` scans only the
> local checkout, so parallel sessions on different branches/checkouts can hand
> out the same ID.

That premise is false for the binaries in use on doudou as of 2026-09-11, and a
substantial doctrine rests on it: fetch before allocating, `erg check` after
every fetch, re-run the seat check at the merge gate, and on collision renumber
**clear of the frontier rather than to the next free ID**. The same document
records what the frontier-jumping rule cost when over-applied — in
search-works-for-zotero it produced allocations at 0050, 0060, 0070 … 0480 in
round decades, "wasting two thirds of its ID space and making the sequence
unreadable".

The harness is paying a parade against a limitation the tool no longer has. The
doctrine is not worthless: a residual window remains, named below. It is
calibrated against the wrong width.

Found while allocating in search-works-for-zotero, where a sibling session held
0766 on a branch. `erg` routed the allocation to 0767 by itself.

## Evidence

Four readings, two of them isolated controls, all doudou 2026-09-11.

1. **The binaries document it themselves.** `strings tickets/erg`: "The
   same-relative subtree of every `refs/heads/` and `refs/remotes/` tip in the
   local refs cache, enumerated via `git for-each-ref` + `git ls-tree`. Catches
   tickets committed on branches not currently checked out", plus a parallel
   clause for "uncommitted drafts in parallel worktrees". Both binaries also
   state the boundary the shorter quote omits: the branch-tip pass has a 200 ms
   deadline and on timeout falls back to the worktree-level result with a
   warning on stderr.

2. **Live routing.** The recorded `find` search over the repository found ticket
   0766 in no working tree; it lived only in a sibling session's branch. `erg
   next-id` returned 0767. The allocator avoided an ID it could only have
   learned from a ref.

3. **Isolated control, the unpushed case.** A local branch was built with
   plumbing (temp `GIT_INDEX_FILE`, so the shared checkout's index and working
   tree were never touched) carrying `tickets/0899-…`, never pushed, no remote
   counterpart:

   | state | `erg next-id` |
   |---|---|
   | baseline | 0768 |
   | unpushed local branch carrying 0899 present | **0900** |
   | probe ref deleted | 0768 |

   0 modified paths in the working tree throughout.

4. **Independent confirmation on the second binary.** The harness build
   (`sha256 53fbf0e0f8e0cd13712ca864da4bdd71efb29b7f8aaf56c8fdce320cbbdcc3fb`,
   revision `4fd3c98`) differs from the search-works-for-zotero build (`sha256
   1e204c1ffd9cd703ac6e778a3b5f3337597accaa3d5ac5c693bf3d8ffb7d9436`,
   revision `5158508`). It carries the same enumeration, and while this ticket
   sat on an unpushed branch as 0926, the harness checkout's `erg next-id`
   returned 0927.

## Two questions the author raised, both answered here

**Does an unpushed sibling branch in a worktree on the same machine get
found?** Yes. Worktrees of one clone share `refs/heads`, and reading 3 shows an
unpushed local branch alone moves the allocation. No forge call is involved.

**Does it fill the holes?** No — it is max+1, never lowest-free. Measured in
search-works-for-zotero: 322 distinct IDs, highest 0767, and **445 free slots
below the maximum**, 58 % of the space. `next-id` returned 0768, not 0038. A gap
below a retained high-water mark is therefore never reused by the current
allocator, which raises the price of the renumber advice above rather than
lowering it.

## Does the harness actually use the allocator?

Mostly yes, which is what makes the correction worth making. `tickets/erg new`
or `$ERG new` is called by `skills/release`, `skills/molt`, `skills/raid`,
`skills/roar`, `skills/trace-doctor`, and `scripts/worktree-exit-preflight.sh`
guards against its uncommitted output.

Two skills mention filing a ticket without pinning the allocator —
`skills/healthcheck` ("create a ticket") and `skills/memory` ("File ticket or
delete"). Neither prescribes hand-picking an ID, so this is a loose end rather
than a defect. The genuinely unguarded path is the collision-recovery advice in
`AGENTS.md` itself, which tells the agent to *choose* a number above the
high-water mark, bypassing the allocator exactly when IDs are most contended.

## The stale claim is repeated elsewhere

`scripts/check-cross-pr-ticket-collision.sh` line 5 carries it again in its
header comment: "`erg new` scans only the local checkout, so two branches can
hand out the same ID". Correcting `AGENTS.md` alone leaves the claim standing in
the script that exists because of it.

## What stays genuinely open

Outside what any local scan can reach:

- a **separate clone** of the same project on the machine, with its own `.git`;
- another machine entirely;
- refs present on the forge but not yet fetched here;
- a sibling that has allocated but neither committed nor left a draft where the
  parallel-worktree scan can see it;
- a branch-tip pass that exceeds its 200 ms deadline and falls back, with a
  warning, to the local and sibling-worktree result; Git enumeration failures
  can likewise reduce ref coverage.

Whether to close any of these, and how, is the "another day" question. Note that
git-erg#282 declined *reservation*, which is a far stronger mechanism than the
read-only scan the binaries now perform, so that wontfix does not settle a
future proposal on its own terms.

## Actions

1. Rewrite the stale bullet in `tickets/AGENTS.md`: say what `erg new` actually
   scans (store subtree, parallel worktrees' uncommitted drafts, and a
   best-effort pass over `refs/heads` and `refs/remotes` tips bounded at 200 ms),
   and name the fallback as well as the residual window of a separate clone or
   unfetched refs.
2. Fix the same claim in `scripts/check-cross-pr-ticket-collision.sh`'s header.
3. Keep the fetch-before-allocating rule. It is exactly what shrinks the
   residual window, and it survives the correction intact.
4. Re-scope the renumber-clear-of-the-frontier rule to the narrowed hazard,
   weighed against its recorded cost and against the max+1 finding above, which
   makes every jumped ID a permanent hole.
5. Re-read the surrounding doctrine for other sentences resting on the same
   premise — the `erg check`-after-every-fetch cadence, the merge-gate seat
   re-check — and say for each whether it still earns its place.
6. Leave the cross-PR CI gate's *behaviour* alone unless step 5 says otherwise:
   it covers the separate-clone and unfetched-refs cases no local scan reaches,
   and in a repo with no CI it is the only check there is.

## Test

Before trusting any inventory of call sites, run the search pattern once
against a case known to be positive. Two greps written for this ticket were
wrong in that exact way: `erg new` missed `trace-doctor`'s `$ERG new`, and a
whitespace-anchored retry dropped `release`'s `tickets/erg new`. A pattern whose
"no hits" is indistinguishable from "I could not look" is not a check.

## Verification

- [ ] Each claim in the corrected bullet is re-derived on the machine rather
      than copied from this ticket, with the binary sha256 recorded beside it.
- [ ] A positive control fires before any "no collision" claim is trusted.

## Invariants

- Do not delete the fetch discipline; the correction narrows the hazard, it does
  not remove it.
- Do not let a binary's strings carry the claim alone. Two of the four readings
  above are behavioural, and those are the ones that settle it.
- `tickets/AGENTS.md` is resident in every session of every project, so every
  sentence added is paid for everywhere. Correct the bullet; do not grow an
  essay around it.

## Exit criteria

`tickets/AGENTS.md` and the collision script describe what the tool actually
does, the collision doctrine is scoped to the residual window, and a reader can
tell which cases remain unprotected and why.

## Note on urgency

Deferred at the author's direction, 2026-09-11. Nothing breaks while it waits.
The cost of waiting is that a resident document tells every session something
false about its tooling, and the frontier-jumping advice it justifies has
already burned two thirds of one project's ID space, permanently, since the
allocator never reuses a hole.
